# A series of isoreticular chiral metal–organic frameworks as a tunable platform for asymmetric catalysis

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks, built by bridging metal ions with organic linkers, represent a new class of porous hybrid materials with attractive tunability in compositions, structures and functions. In particular, the mild conditions typically employed for their synthesis allow for the functionalization of their building blocks, and thus the rational design of novel materials. Here we demonstrate the systematic design of eight mesoporous chiral metal-organic frameworks, with the framework formula [LCu2(solvent)2] (where L is a chiral tetracarboxylate ligand derived from 1,1'-bi-2-naphthol), that have the same structures but channels of different sizes. Chiral Lewis acid catalysts were generated by postsynthesis functionalization with Ti(OiPr)4, and the resulting materials proved to be highly active asymmetric catalysts for diethylzinc and alkynylzinc additions, which converted aromatic aldehydes into chiral secondary alcohols. The enantioselectivities of these reactions can be modified by tuning the size of the channels, which alters the diffusion rates of the organic substrates.

Metal–organic frameworks, built by bridging metal ions with organic linkers, represent a new class of porous hybrid materials with attractive tunability in compositions, structures and functions. In particular, the mild conditions typically employed for their synthesis allow for the functionalization of their building blocks, and thus the rational design of novel materials. Here we demonstrate the systematic design of eight mesoporous chiral metal–organic frameworks, with the framework formula $\mathbf{[LCu}_{2}(\mathsf{s o l v e n t})_{2}\mathbf{]}$ (where L is a chiral tetracarboxylate ligand derived from 1,1′-bi-2-naphthol), that have the same structures but channels of different sizes. Chiral Lewis acid catalysts were generated by postsynthesis functionalization with ${\overline{{\operatorname{Ii}}}}(\mathbf{O}^{i}\mathbf{Pr})_{4},$ and the resulting materials proved to be highly active asymmetric catalysts for diethylzinc and alkynylzinc additions, which converted aromatic aldehydes into chiral secondary alcohols. The enantioselectivities of these reactions can be modified by tuning the size of the channels, which alters the diffusion rates of the organic substrates.
uring the past few years, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as an interesting class of hybrid materials with potential applications in many diverse areas, including gas storage1–3, chemical sensing4–6, catalysis7–9, biomedical imagin g10–12 and drug delivery13,14. Unlike traditional inorganic materials, typically MOFs are prepared under mild conditions, which allow the rational and tunable design of functional materials by judicious incorporation of constituent building blocks with the desired functionalities. Unlike organic polymers, MOFs are highly crystalline and their structures can be characterized readily by X-ray diffraction, which facilitates the elucidation of their structure– property relationships. MOFs thus present a unique platform for the design of functional materials using both molecular and crystal engineering by combining highly versatile organic synthetic methodologies and well-established strategies for the synthesis of inorganic solid-state materials.
Thus far the systematic engineering of highly porous MOFs for gas capture and storage has attracted the most interest, and the strategies used to design MOFs with unprecedentedly high microporosity (with pore or channel openings less than $1\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) and gas-uptake capacities are now relatively well-established1–3,15,16. However, it remains a significant challenge to synthesize MOFs with mesoporosity (with pore or channel openings of several nanometres) because of their tendency to reduce or eliminate porosity through interpenetration or other void-filling means17–19. Chiral mesoporous MOFs with large open channels and pores are even more scarce9,20,21, which severely limits their applications in asymmetric catalysis to generate the chiral molecules essential for biological processes.
MOFs provide an ideal platform for designing heterogenized asymmetric catalysts because of the ability to create uniform active catalytic sites with identical secondary environments around them throughout the solid. Also, MOFs can have unprecedentedly high catalyst loadings, more accessible catalytic centres and prolonged catalyst lifetimes because of the elimination of multimolecular catalyst-deactivation pathways. Precise knowledge of MOF structures also facilitates the identification of active catalyst sites, and thereby simplifies the elucidation of the catalytic mechanisms, which can be very difficult to unravel for traditional heterogeneous catalysts. For asymmetric catalytic reactions, it is imperative to design MOFs with large open channels because of the need to transport typically very large organic substrates and products. Furthermore, the MOFs must have well-defined asymmetric catalytic sites or chiral pores and channels to differentiate the generation of two possible enantiomers from a prochiral substrate. Although scattered examples of asymmetric catalysis with chiral MOFs (CMOFs) have appeared in recent years20–25, no study has yet taken advantage of the ability to engineer systematically CMOFs with tunable functionalities and open channel sizes. Here we report the rational design of a family of isoreticular mesoporous MOFs and their postsynthetic functionalization for applications in enantioselective catalysis (Fig. 1a).
A series of tetracarboxylic acids that contained orthogonal chiral diethoxy (L1a to L4a) or dihydroxy (L1b to L4b) functional groups in the $(R)$ -enantiomeric form were synthesized for this work (Fig. 1b). We envisaged that metal-connecting units can link these chiral bridging ligands to form CMOFs by means of the carboxylate primary functional groups. The orthogonal chiral dihydroxy secondary functionalities in ligands L1b to L4b that periodically decorate the porous CMOF can then be postsynthetically functionalized to generate asymmetric catalytic sites that are accessible to the organic substrates through the open channels or cavities. In the proof-of-concept experiments, we chose carboxylate-bridged copper paddle-wheels as the targeted secondary building units (SBUs) to link the axially chiral bridging ligands to form CMOFs. Recently, this combination of building blocks was used to construct stable 4,4-connected MOFs of the PtS and related topology26–30.
## Results and discussion
Blue tetragonal bipyramid crystals of CMOFs with the tetracarboxylate bridging ligands were grown by solvothermal reactions between $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and the tetracarboxylic acid in diethylformamide (DEF)– $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixed solvents at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Single-crystal X-ray structure determinations revealed the framework formula of $\mathrm{[\mathbf{L}C u_{2}(s o l v e n t)_{2}]}$ for CMOFs made with all the tetracarboxylic acids (solvent $\c=$ water or DEF; see Table 1 for the detailed characterization of the CMOF series). Repeated crystallization attempts with various $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ salts and L1b failed to produce crystals suitable for single-crystal X-ray diffraction, but powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) studies indicated that the polycrystalline product CMOF-1b obtained from the reaction carried out in the DEF– $\mathbf{\cdotH}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ mixed solvents is isostructural to CMOF-1a. The phase purity for each of the eight CMOFs was confirmed by an excellent match between the experimental and simulated PXRD patterns.
All of the eight CMOFs are isostructural and adopt a threedimensional (3D) non-interpenetrating framework structure that crystallizes in the same chiral space group $I4_{1}22$ . The structure of
CMOF-3a was described briefly in an earlier communication29. We describe only the structure of CMOF-1a in detail here. The asymmetric unit of CMOF-1a contains one Cu atom, one halfligand L1a and one coordinated DEF for the framework. The Cu atom is coordinated by four carboxylate oxygen atoms of four different L1a ligands in the equatorial positions and one DEF molecule in the axial position to give a square pyramidal geometry. The resulting copper paddle-wheel $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]$ SBUs are interconnected by the L1a ligand to form a 3D network (Fig. 2). Although the carboxylate groups in the $^{4,4^{'}}$ -positions reside at either side of the rotational axis of the binaphthyl scaffold, those in the $6{,}6^{'}$ -positions are twisted from each other with a dihedral angle of $82.7^{\circ}$ for the two naphthyl rings. The resulting framework of CMOF-1a adopts a 4,4-connected network topology that can be described by the Schla¨fli symbol $\{4^{3};6^{2};8\}$ . As a result of the elongated tetracarboxylate ligand, the non-interpenetrating framework of CMOF-1a that is produced possesses large open channels that run through all three directions of the crystals, with the largest opening of $1.3\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ along the $a/b$ and $c$ axes, respectively (Fig. 2).
Interestingly, the 4,4-connected network topology observed for CMOF-1a is very robust and accommodates tetracarboxylic acid ligands of varying sizes as well as both diethoxy (CMOF-1a to -4a) and dihydroxy (CMOF-1b to -4b) functional groups (Table 1). As a result of the different lengths of the tetracarboxylate ligands, the CMOF series is highly porous with a tunable void space that ranges from $73.3\%$ to $91.9\%$ , as calculated by PLATON for the CMOF-1b to -4b series (Table 1)31. The ethoxy-protected CMOF-1a to -4a series has a void space that ranges from $68.5\%$ to $90.0\%$ . As shown in Fig. 3, the CMOF-1b to -4b series has tunable channel openings of $1.3\times1.1\mathrm{nm}$ to $3.2\times2.4~\mathrm{nm}$ along the $a/b$ axis and from $0.8\times0.8~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $2.1\times2.1$ nm along the $c$ axis. As expected, the CMOF-1a to -4a series has slightly smaller open channels because of the steric bulk of the ethyl groups. The calculated framework density for the CMOFs ranges from 0.730 to $0.177\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . With the chiral dihydroxy groups pointing towards the open channels, the CMOF-1b to -4b series provides an ideal platform for generating heterogeneous catalysts by grafting a secondary metal centre by postsynthetic modifications (PSMs; see below) $\phantom{-}22,32\dot{-}34$ . As a result of their highly disordered nature, it is difficult to determine and locate precisely the included solvent molecules in the CMOFs by X-ray crystallography. We used a combination of $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) to establish the solvent contents in the CMOFs (Table 1 gives details of the solvent contents). TGA analyses showed that the CMOF-1b to $\mathbf{-4b}$ series exhibited an impressive solvent weight loss of 58.4 weight per cent $(\mathrm{wt\%})$ to $73.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in the temperature range $25\mathrm{-}200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Table 1), which confirmed their highly open framework structures. Consistent with the bulkier ethyl groups, the CMOF-1a to -4a series exhibited a solvent weight loss of $40.3\mathrm{wt\%}$ to $69.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in the same temperature range (Table 1).
Although thousands of MOFs are described in the literature of the past decade, relatively few examples of isoreticular MOFs are reported35,36. The isoreticular MOF family based on the 6-connected $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{6}]$ SBUs and 2-connected linear dicarboxylate bridging ligands of different lengths represents the most well-established isoreticular porous materials37. A large number of isoreticular MOFs and analogues were synthesized and exhibited tunable pore and/or channel sizes, shapes and functionalities. With enhanced stability over the $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{6}]$ SBUs, carboxylate-bridged copper paddle-wheels represent another interesting SBU for the synthesis of isoreticular $\mathrm{MOF}s^{38}$ . Recently, we and others demonstrated the ability to construct 4,4-connected MOFs of PtS and related topology based on copper paddle-wheels and tetracarboxylate bridging ligands $26-30$ . Given the highly elongated structures of the tetracarboxylate ligands, it was surprising to observe the formation of non-interpenetrating structures in all of the CMOFs. We believe that the steric bulk of both the copper paddle-wheel SBUs and the $^{4,4^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}}$ -substituted ${1,1}^{\prime}.$ -bi-2-naphthol (BINOL)-derived ligands prevents the CMOFs from adopting interpenetrating structures39,40. This work highlights the ability to engineer isoreticular CMOFs with tunable open channel sizes and functional groups by using chiral tetracarboxylate ligands of different lengths.
As shown in Table 1 and Fig. 4a, Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations indicated that the CMOFs should be highly porous with Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas that ranged from 2,418 to $\ 4{,}514\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (ref. 41). However, nitrogenadsorption measurements showed that the CMOFs exhibited only modest porosity with BET surface areas that ranged from 0 to $343~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The very large discrepancy between the calculated and experimental surface areas probably results from the framework distortion on solvent removal. The ‘breathing effect’ is quite common among porous $\mathrm{MOFs^{42,43}}$ , particularly for those with very large open channels, such as the CMOF series described here. PXRD studies confirmed the severe framework distortion for the CMOFs on solvent removal; most of the diffraction peaks disappeared for the evacuated CMOF samples. After soaking in DMF– $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and ${\mathrm{DEF}}{\mathrm{-H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ for several hours, the evacuated samples of the CMOFs regained the PXRD patterns of pristine CMOFs. This result is consistent with framework distortion during solvent removal. Recent reports indicated that MOF porosity can be better preserved when solvents are removed by gentler means, such as supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ processing44 or freeze-drying40. Numerous attempts with freeze-drying, however, failed to show any enhancement of the BET surface areas of CMOFs.
X-ray diffraction studies indicated that the elongated tetracarboxylate bridging ligands used in this work led to the mesoporosity that is required in CMOFs for asymmetric catalysis because of the need to transport very large reagent and product molecules. The large channels and pores cause severe framework distortion of these MOFs on solvent removal, and thus present a significant challenge in determining and quantifying their porosity. The gasadsorption experiments typically used to determine porosity gave meaninglessly small surface areas and pore sizes for the CMOFs; the gas-adsorption measurements of the CMOF series gave less than $5\%$ of the BET surface areas predicted from GCMC simulations of the desolvated CMOFs.
We developed a straightforward dye-uptake assay to access the capability of the CMOFs to transport large reagent and product molecules through the open channels by taking advantage of the hydrophobic nature of the open channels. We carried out dye-uptake studies by soaking the CMOFs in a solution of Brilliant Blue R-250 (BBR-250) dye in methanol for 24 hours. The dye solution was decanted and the CMOFs were washed with water several times to remove dye molecules adsorbed on the external surfaces of the crystals. We hypothesized that water cannot readily enter the hydrophobic channels and wash away included dye molecules. The dyeloaded CMOFs were then digested with sodium ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid $(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA})$ and the amounts of released BBR-250 quantified by ultraviolet–visible spectroscopy. As shown in Table 1 and Fig. 4b, remarkable size selectivity was observed for the dye uptake: CMOF-1a and -1b had only negligible uptake of BBR-250, probably because the molecular dimensions of BBR-250 exceed the open channel sizes. In contrast, CMOF-2a to -4b had very significant dye uptake, equivalent to as much as $124\%$ of the framework weight. The dye-uptake experiment thus demonstrates conclusively the accessibility of the potential active catalyst sites to the organic substrates through the open channels. The remarkable difference in dye uptake among the CMOF series also provides indirect evidence for the retention of the framework structure in solution. Such a size selectivity of dye uptake would not be expected if the CMOF framework had decomposed. More importantly, the dye-uptake experiments provide a predictive tool for the catalytic performances of the CMOFs (see below). We believe that the dye-uptake experiment should prove valuable in the characterization of other mesoporous MOFs with large open channels and pores.
Enantioselective catalysis. The results from GCMC calculations and dye-uptake experiments indicate strongly that significant void space exists in the CMOF series and should be accessible to large organic molecules. The chiral dihydroxy groups in CMOF-1b to -4b are particularly attractive for further functionalization with a secondary metal centre to give active catalysts. Ti(IV) complexes of BINOL, for example, were shown to catalyse a number of highly enantioselective reactions, such as the addition of diethylzinc or alkynylzinc to aromatic aldehydes, to provide a wide range of chiral secondary alcohols (on hydrolytic work-up)45. We believe that $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ can react with the chiral dihydroxy groups in the CMOFs to give Lewis acidic (BINOLate) $\mathrm{\dot{Ti}}(\mathrm{\bar{O}}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{2}$ compounds that are grafted onto the frameworks of the CMOFs and potentially are accessible to the aromatic aldehydes and diethylzinc or alkynylzinc reagents (Fig. 5a). Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometric analysis indicated that a sample of CMOF-3b treated with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ had a Ti/BINOL ratio of $(1.48\pm$ 0.10):1, which suggests the formation of (BINOLate) $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{2}$ compounds within CMOF-3b. The $\mathrm{Ti(O^{{i}}P r)_{4}}$ -treated CMOF-3b exhibited an identical PXRD to that of the pristine CMOF-3b, which indicates maintenance of its framework structure on ${\mathrm{Ti}}({\mathrm{IV}})$ loading (Fig. 5b).
We investigated the asymmetric catalytic activity of the functionalized CMOFs by studying the diethylzinc and alkynylzinc additions to aromatic aldehydes. CMOF-3b was used to screen for diethylzinc addition reaction conditions (Fig. 6, equation (1)). Treatment of CMOF-3b with excess $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ led to active catalysts for the addition of diethylzinc to a range of aromatic aldehydes with almost complete conversions (up to ${>}99\%$ ), high selectivity for secondary alcohols (up to $99\%$ ) and high enantiomeric excess (e.e., up to $91\%$ for 1-naphthaldehyde) (Table 2, entries 1–5). This level of enantioselectivity is very good for heterogeneous catalysts, and is as little as $3\%$ lower than that of the homogeneous (BINOLate) $\mathrm{\Ti(O^{{i}}P r)}_{2}$ catalyst (Table 2, entries 6–10). The diethylzinc addition results observed for CMOF-3b are comparable to those of a chiral bipyridine–Cd MOF-derived catalyst22. Control experiments showed that CMOF-3a without the chiral dihydroxy groups in combination with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ or $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ alone is capable of catalysing the diethylzinc addition reaction with no enantioselectivity (Table 2, entry 14). The slightly lower enantiomeric excesses observed in the CMOF series described here (as compared to the homogeneous catalyst) probably result from the competing nonenantioselective background reaction.
To prove the lack of CMOF catalytic activity in the supernatant of the CMOF-3b/ $\mathrm{Ti(O^{{i}}P r)_{4}}$ system, we added 4-chlorobenzaldehyde to the supernatant of the diethylzinc-to-benzaldehyde addition reaction mixture. After the addition of $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ and diethylzinc to the supernatant, the diethylzinc-to-4-chlorobenzaldehyde addition product, 1- ( $\boldsymbol{p}$ -chlorophenyl)-1-propanol, was totally racemic. The enantiomeric excess for 1-phenyl-1-propanol also dropped from $70.0\%$ to $18.2\%$ in the supernatant. The formation of totally racemic 1-( $p$ -chlorophenyl)-1-propanol in the cross-over experiment shows that the supernatant exhibited no CMOF catalytic activity for the diethylzinc addition reactions, which supports the heterogeneous nature of the CMOF-3b/ $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ catalyst. This was further confirmed by catalyst reuse experiments with the CMOF-3b catalyst; we were able to recover and reuse CMOF-3b for the diethylzinc addition to benzaldehyde without deterioration of conversion, selectivity and enantiomeric excess up to five times (Table 2, entries 18–23). Furthermore, the solid catalyst recovered from the catalytic diethylzinc addition reaction exhibited the same PXRD as the pristine solid of CMOF3b (Fig. 5b), which supports the stability of the CMOF framework during the catalytic reactions.
