To examine the generality of this PSM strategy towards asymmetric catalysis with CMOFs, we also carried out the addition of alkynylzinc to aromatic aldehydes to generate chiral alcohols (Fig. 6, equation (2)). Screening experiments showed that the CMOF-3b and ${\mathrm{Ti}}(\mathrm{O}^{i}{\mathrm{Pr}})_{4}$ combination is a highly active catalyst for the addition of ethyl(phenylalkynyl)zinc to a wide range of aromatic aldehydes to give chiral alcohols with complete conversions and up to $76\%$ e.e (Table 3). The solid catalyst recovered from the alkynylzinc addition reaction exhibited the same PXRD as the pristine solid of CMOF-3b (Fig. 5b), which demonstrates that the CMOF framework is stable under the catalytic alkynylzinc addition reaction conditions. Control experiments with CMOF-3a also indicated a significant background reaction catalysed by the non-chiral active sites to give the racemic alcohols. The lower enantiomeric excess values observed for the CMOF-3b/ $\mathrm{\ddot{Ti}(O^{\dot{2}}P r)_{4}}$ catalyst as compared to the homogeneous catalyst probably arose from the competing non-enantioselective background reaction46. It is interesting that the reaction with 1-naphthaldehyde gave a much lower enantiomeric excess than that with the other aromatic aldehydes, presumably because of the hindered diffusion of the large aldehyde and alkynylzinc reagents.
We also carried out the addition of ethyl(phenylalkynyl)zinc to benzaldehyde catalysed by heterogeneous catalysts derived from CMOF-1b, -2b and -4b. No enantioselectivity was observed for the reactions carried out with both CMOF-1b and CMOF-2b, which supports the notion that the alkynylzinc addition reaction requires larger open channels because of the larger sizes of the reagents. Consistent with this, the $\mathrm{{\bfCMOF{-}4b/T i(O^{i}P r)_{4}}}$ catalyst gave slightly enhanced enantioselectivity $77\%$ e.e. for benzaldehyde and $49\%$ e.e. for 1-naphthaldehyde) than the CMOF-3b/ $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\dot{\mathrm{Pr}})_{4}$ catalyst ( $76\%$ e.e. for benzaldehyde and $31\%$ e.e. for 1-naphthaldehyde). These results demonstrate that the catalytic performances of the CMOF-derived catalysts are dependent on the sizes of the open channels because of the need to transport large reagent and product molecules.
## Conclusions
A family of isoreticular CMOFs was constructed from copper paddle-wheels and BINOL-derived tetracarboxylic acid bridging ligands. These CMOFs have the same non-interpenetrating framework structures but different open channel sizes, and thus provide an ideal platform for generating heterogeneous catalysts by postsynthetic grafting of the Ti(IV) complex onto the chiral dihydroxy groups. The $\bar{\mathrm{CMOF/Ti}}({\mathrm{O}}^{i}{\mathrm{Pr}})_{4}$ combinations are highly active catalysts for the additions of diethylzinc and ethyl(phenylalkynyl)zinc to a wide range of aromatic aldehydes to afford chiral secondary alcohols. The framework structure of the CMOF-derived catalysts is stable under the catalytic reaction conditions. Furthermore, the enantiomeric excesses of these reactions were found to be dependent on the open channel sizes because of the competition between the enantioselective (BINOLate)Ti $\mathrm{(O^{{i}}P r)}_{2}$ -catalysed reaction and the non-enantioselective background reaction and because of the different diffusion rates of the organic substrates through the open channels of varied sizes. Isoreticular CMOFs thus represent an ideal tunable platform for designing heterogenized asymmetric catalysts that have uniform active catalytic sites, identical secondary environments, and controllable open channel and pore sizes.
## Methods
General procedure for dye-uptake measurements. Fresh crystals of CMOF-4b (3.22 mg, $9.1\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ ) were dried briefly on a filter paper, and then soaked in a methanol solution of BBR-250 $24~\mathrm{mM}$ , $2~\mathrm{ml}$ ) overnight. The resulting blue crystals were washed with water thoroughly until the washings become colourless. The washed samples were digested by $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA}$ (0.05 M, $2~\mathrm{ml}$ ) and NaOH (6 M, $0.1\mathrm{ml}$ ), the resultant clear solution with light blue colour was diluted to $25~\mathrm{ml}$ and adjusted to a $\mathrm{\pH}$ of 1.2. Absorption experiments were performed on a Shimadzu UV-2401PC ultraviolet–visible spectrophotometer. The concentration of BBR-250 was determined by comparing the ultraviolet–visible absorption with a standard curve.
General procedure for CMOF $\mathbf{\ddot{\Gamma}}\mathbf{\dot{n}}(\mathbf{O}^{i}\mathbf{Pr})_{4}$ -catalysed diethylzinc addition reactions. Fresh crystals of CMOF-3b (12.57 mg, $4.94~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\dot{$ ) were washed with MeOH and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ repeatedly, and then sealed and brought into a nitrogen-filled glovebox. The solvent was decanted and $\mathrm{~1~ml}$ of dry toluene added to the sample. $\bar{\mathrm{Ti}}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ ( $31.6~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ , $110\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ ) was added to the suspension and stirred for one hour. Benzaldehyde $(3.7\upmu1,36.9\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ and diethylzinc in toluene ( ${\mathrm{90~}}\upmu\updownarrow,$ 1.1 M, $98.9\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ) were added sequentially to the stirring suspension and allowed to react overnight. The reaction mixture was quenched by the addition of $\mathrm{1~ml}$ of 3M HCl. The organic layer was passed down a short silica column and analysed by chiral gas chromatography to give conversion, selectivity and enantiomeric excess values.
General procedure for CMOF/ $\mathbf{\ddot{\Pi}}\mathbf{Ti}(\mathbf{O}^{i}\mathbf{Pr})_{4}.$ -catalysed ethyl(phenylalkynyl)zinc addition reactions. The solvent-exchanged CMOF-3b (12.57 mg, $4.94~\upmu\mathrm{mol}\$ ) was sealed and brought into a nitrogen-filled glovebox. The solvent was decanted and $\mathrm{~1~ml}$ of dry toluene added to the sample. $\mathrm{\bar{Ti}(O^{\i}P r)_{4}}$ $[10.07~\upmu],$ $30\ \upmu\mathrm{mol};$ ) and $\mathrm{Zn(Et)(CCPh)}$ (17.6 mg, $90~\mathrm{\upmumol}$ ) were added sequentially and the mixture was stirred for one hour. Benzaldehyde $1.5~\upmu\mathrm{l}.$ , $14.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ) was added and the reaction mixture stirred overnight. The reaction was quenched with saturated ammonium chloride $(1.5~\mathrm{ml})$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA}$ $\mathrm{1.5~ml})$ ). The resulting mixture was stirred for 30 minutes. The organic layer was washed with water and the solvent removed in vacuo. The residual oil was then dissolved in $10\%$ $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ isopropanol in hexanes and analysed by chiral high-performance liquid chromatography to give enantiomeric excess values.
